{"step_index":2446,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T15:59:17Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T11:59:17-04:00\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T11:59:17-04:00\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/indexedDbStorage.ts`\nTotal Lines: 221\nTotal Bytes: 6744\nShowing lines 1 to 160\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: /**\n2:  * High-Capacity IndexedDB Storage Driver for Web Browsers\n3:  * Solves the 5MB browser localStorage limit and provides multi-gigabyte, crash-proof persistence\n4:  * for ValuTeachers leads, photos, call records, and notes.\n5:  */\n6: \n7: const DB_NAME = 'SnapDialerValuTeachersDB';\n8: const DB_VERSION = 1;\n9: const STORE_NAME = 'app_state';\n10: const LEGACY_STORAGE_KEY = 'snap_dialer_valuteachers_data';\n11: \n12: export interface DatabaseState {\n13:   lists: any[];\n14:   leads: any[];\n15:   call_logs: any[];\n16:   cadence_rules: any[];\n17:   sms_templates: any[];\n18:   settings: { key: string; value: string }[];\n19: }\n20: \n21: export class IndexedDbStorage {\n22:   private static dbPromise: Promise<IDBDatabase> | null = null;\n23: \n24:   /**\n25:    * Opens or initializes the IndexedDB database connection\n26:    */\n27:   private static async getDb(): Promise<IDBDatabase> {\n28:     if (typeof window === 'undefined' || !window.indexedDB) {\n29:       throw new Error('IndexedDB not supported in current environment.');\n30:     }\n31: \n32:     if (!this.dbPromise) {\n33:       this.dbPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n34:         const request = window.indexedDB.open(DB_NAME, DB_VERSION);\n35: \n36:         request.onupgradeneeded = (event: any) => {\n37:           const db = event.target.result as IDBDatabase;\n38:           if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains(STORE_NAME)) {\n39:             db.createObjectStore(STORE_NAME);\n40:           }\n41:         };\n42: \n43:         request.onsuccess = (event: any) => {\n44:           const db = event.target.result as IDBDatabase;\n45: \n46:           // Request persistent storage if supported by browser\n47:           if (navigator.storage && navigator.storage.persist) {\n48:             navigator.storage.persist().catch(() => {});\n49:           }\n50: \n51:           resolve(db);\n52:         };\n53: \n54:         request.onerror = (event: any) => {\n55:           console.error('IndexedDB open error:', event.target.error);\n56:           reject(event.target.error);\n57:         };\n58:       });\n59:     }\n60: \n61:     return this.dbPromise;\n62:   }\n63: \n64:   /**\n65:    * Loads the full database state from IndexedDB (with automatic migration from legacy localStorage)\n66:    */\n67:   static async loadState(): Promise<DatabaseState> {\n68:     try {\n69:       if (typeof window === 'undefined' || !window.indexedDB) {\n70:         return this.loadFromLocalStorageFallback();\n71:       }\n72: \n73:       const db = await this.getDb();\n74:       const stored = await new Promise<DatabaseState | null>((resolve, reject) => {\n75:         try {\n76:           const tx = db.transaction(STORE_NAME, 'readonly');\n77:           const store = tx.objectStore(STORE_NAME);\n78:           const request = store.get('root_state');\n79: \n80:           request.onsuccess = () => {\n81:             resolve(request.result || null);\n82:           };\n83: \n84:           request.onerror = () => {\n85:             reject(request.error);\n86:           };\n87:         } catch (e) {\n88:           reject(e);\n89:         }\n90:       });\n91: \n92:       if (stored) {\n93:         return this.normalizeState(stored);\n94:       }\n95: \n96:       // Check if we have legacy data in localStorage to migrate\n97:       const legacyState = this.loadFromLocalStorageFallback();\n98:       if (legacyState.leads.length > 0 || legacyState.lists.length > 0) {\n99:         await this.saveState(legacyState);\n100:         return legacyState;\n101:       }\n102: \n103:       return {\n104:         lists: [],\n105:         leads: [],\n106:         call_logs: [],\n107:         cadence_rules: [],\n108:         sms_templates: [],\n109:         settings: [],\n110:       };\n111:     } catch (e) {\n112:       console.warn('IndexedDB load failed, using localStorage fallback:', e);\n113:       return this.loadFromLocalStorageFallback();\n114:     }\n115:   }\n116: \n117:   /**\n118:    * Persists the entire database state to IndexedDB\n119:    */\n120:   static async saveState(state: DatabaseState): Promise<void> {\n121:     try {\n122:       if (typeof window === 'undefined' || !window.indexedDB) {\n123:         this.saveToLocalStorageFallback(state);\n124:         return;\n125:       }\n126: \n127:       const db = await this.getDb();\n128:       await new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {\n129:         try {\n130:           const tx = db.transaction(STORE_NAME, 'readwrite');\n131:           const store = tx.objectStore(STORE_NAME);\n132:           const request = store.put(state, 'root_state');\n133: \n134:           request.onsuccess = () => {\n135:             resolve();\n136:           };\n137: \n138:           request.onerror = () => {\n139:             reject(request.error);\n140:           };\n141:         } catch (e) {\n142:           reject(e);\n143:         }\n144:       });\n145: \n146:       // Also keep a lightweight mirror in localStorage (without large base64 images) as a secondary backup\n147:       this.saveToLocalStorageFallback(state, true);\n148:     } catch (e) {\n149:       console.error('IndexedDB save failed:', e);\n150:       this.saveToLocalStorageFallback(state, true);\n151:     }\n152:   }\n153: \n154:   /**\n155:    * Normalizes fields for backwards compatibility\n156:    */\n157:   private static normalizeState(raw: any): DatabaseState {\n158:     const leads = (Array.isArray(raw?.leads) ? raw.leads : []).map((l: any) => ({\n159:       ...l,\n160:       priority_tier: l.priority_tier !== undefined && l.priority_tier !== null ? Number(l.priority_tier) : 1,\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":2447,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T15:59:17Z","content":"### Comprehensive Production Audit: IndexedDB Architecture, Performance & Scalability\n\nHere is a technical assessment of how the storage layer is structured, where it stands against industry standards, and an evaluation of each optimization technique.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Architectural Review of the Current Engine\n\nCurrently, SnapDialer uses a **Hybrid In-Memory + IndexedDB Snapshot Driver** ([`indexedDbStorage.ts`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/indexedDbStorage.ts) & [`database.ts`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/database.ts)):\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        CURRENT STORAGE TOPOLOGY                        │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. Memory Tier (WebDatabase)  ➔ Queries run in sub-millisecond RAM    │\n│ 2. IndexedDB (app_state Store) ➔ Full atomic snapshot persistence      │\n│ 3. LocalStorage Mirror         ➔ Lightweight emergency fallback         │\n│ 4. Storage Eviction Guard      ➔ navigator.storage.persist() active    │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n- **How it performs now:** For typical field pipelines (100 to 5,000 educator leads and proof card images), querying in RAM is fast ($<2\\text{ms}$), while `IndexedDbStorage` ensures multi-gigabyte capacity beyond the 5MB `localStorage` ceiling.\n- **Where the bottlenecks emerge at scale:** When the pipeline exceeds 10,000+ leads or dozens of high-res base64 field card images (50MB+), saving the entire state tree as a single monolithic JSON snapshot key (`'root_state'`) creates CPU serialization overhead on writes.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive on Your Specific Optimizations\n\n#### A. Batching Operations vs. Single `.put()` Loops\n* **Industry Standard:** Never iterate with `for (...) await store.put(item)` because each call incurs separate event-loop microtask turns and transaction locking overhead.\n* **Our Current State:** Because we save the state snapshot in one call (`store.put(state, 'root_state')`), we do not execute single `.put()` loops.\n* **Production Recommendation for Granular Stores:** If transitioning from a monolithic snapshot to normalized tables (`leads`, `call_logs`, `lists`), use a single `readwrite` transaction with batched cursor writes or `store.put()` calls within a single transaction scope.\n\n#### B. Worker Thread Offloading (WebWorkers)\n* **Performance Impact:** Offloading heavy sorting, OCR image decompression, and multi-column filtering to a dedicated background `Worker` keeps the main thread dedicated to 60–120 FPS UI animations.\n* **Assessment for SnapDialer:** \n  - For active dialing HUD and contact viewing ($<5{,}000$ leads), modern JavaScript V8/JSC engines filter in RAM in $\\approx 1\\text{--}3\\text{ms}$, which does not drop frames.\n  - However, for **Bulk CSV Roster Ingestion** (parsing 2,000+ rows) and **Camera OCR processing**, worker offloading prevents any stutter on mobile devices.\n\n#### C. Relaxed Durability (`{ durability: 'relaxed' }`)\n* **How it works:** In Chromium/Blink engines, passing `{ durability: 'relaxed' }` to `IDBDatabase.transaction()` tells the operating system not to force an immediate synchronous disk `fsync` flush after every transaction, but to allow the OS to write in batch.\n* **Speedup:** Yields a **3x to 10x throughput improvement** during heavy batch operations (e.g., bulk roster importing).\n* **Browser Compatibility:** Chromium/Chrome/Edge support this natively; Safari (WebKit) and Firefox gracefully ignore the option without throwing errors.\n\n#### D. Persistent Storage Guard (`navigator.storage.persist()`)\n* **Current Status:** **Already active** in [`indexedDbStorage.ts`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/indexedDbStorage.ts#L46-L50).\n* **Mobile iOS PWA Consideration:** On iOS Safari, web apps run under a 7-day data eviction rule for standard Safari tabs. **However, once the user installs SnapDialer to their Home Screen as a standalone PWA**, iOS grants permanent persistent storage privileges that are exempt from the 7-day browser cache cleanup.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Alternative Engine Consideration: SQLite WASM over OPFS\n\n| Feature | Raw IndexedDB | SQLite WASM + OPFS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Query Flexibility** | Key-value / Object index scans | Full ANSI SQL, Subqueries, Complex Joins |\n| **Write Performance** | Fast async object storage | Direct synchronous byte-level filesystem IO |\n| **Web / PWA Compatibility** | 100% universal (iOS Safari, Android, Web) | Requires COOP/COEP HTTP headers for SharedArrayBuffer |\n| **Static CDN Deployment** | Zero configuration (Works on Surge, Netlify, Vercel) | Complex (Surge cannot easily inject required COOP/COEP headers) |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> While SQLite WASM + OPFS is great for complex multi-table SQL queries, its strict requirement for `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` and `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp` headers makes it challenging to host on static PWA domains like `surge.sh`. IndexedDB remains the most reliable cross-platform web database.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Evaluation of Dexie.js\n\n**Dexie.js** is a minimalist, battle-tested wrapper around IndexedDB ($<25\\text{kB}$).\n\n#### Why Dexie.js would improve stability:\n1. **Granular Multi-Table Storage:** Instead of saving a single monolithic state snapshot, Dexie provides native table schemas:\n   ```typescript\n   db.version(1).stores({\n     leads: 'id, list_id, school, priority_tier, last_disposition, phone',\n     lists: 'id, title',\n     call_logs: 'id, lead_id, timestamp'\n   });\n   ```\n2. **Built-in `bulkPut` & `bulkAdd`:** Automatically optimizes bulk writes into single-transaction chunked batches.\n3. **Compound & B-Tree Indexing:** Queries like `db.leads.where('school').equals('DeLand High').and(l => l.priority_tier === 1)` leverage binary indexes rather than full in-memory table scans.\n4. **Browser Workarounds:** Handles internal Safari WebKit IndexedDB transaction-closing bugs and connection leaks automatically.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Summary & Strategic Recommendation\n\n1. **Current Codebase State:** The current engine is resilient, crash-safe, and capable for field dialing.\n2. **For Future Scalability (10,000+ Leads):** Upgrading the storage layer to **Dexie.js** with normalized table stores, compound indexes on `school` / `priority_tier`, and `{ durability: 'relaxed' }` on bulk imports provides the most scalable, production-grade foundation while maintaining 100% universal PWA compatibility across all mobile devices.","thinking":"**Assessing the Architecture**\n\nOkay, I've started delving into the user's points. I'm focusing on the current architecture and how `IndexedDbStorage` interacts with the `DatabaseState` object. It seems the whole snapshot is being persisted, and I need to determine if there are any inefficiencies there.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Persistence Method**\n\nI've been examining how the data persists. Currently, a single key, 'root\\_state', within the 'app\\_state' object store handles everything. We're already using `navigator.storage.persist()`. The current setup in `WebDatabase` essentially commits a snapshot to `IndexedDbStorage`, which is essentially a single `.put()` operation. However, I'm thinking about the implications if we have a lot of leads, and how that could impact performance. A single store might not scale well.\n\n\n**Exploring Optimization Strategies**\n\nI'm now considering industry best practices for data persistence and identifying potential optimizations. Using range writes, or `bulkPut` operations within a single transaction, seems promising for granular updates. Offloading CPU-intensive tasks like OCR or CSV parsing to a WebWorker would prevent UI blocking. I'm also considering the implications of using relaxed durability for batch imports and how we already have persistent storage.\n\n\n**Evaluating Storage Options**\n\nI'm now diving into alternative storage options. Specifically, the permanent storage rights granted to PWAs on iOS. I'm exploring querying `navigator.storage.persisted()` and `navigator.storage.estimate()` for storage diagnostics, which I could display in Settings. I'm also considering SQLite WASM over OPFS, weighing its pros like true SQL and cons, such as OPFS header requirements. Dexie.js is also under consideration.\n\n\n"}
